---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const href = (value: string) => `${base.replace(/\/?$/, '/')}${value.replace(/^\/+/, '')}`;
const flow = 'bench-suite: Package / Case / RunVariant / optional Suite\n' +
  '                         │\n' +
  '                         ▼\n' +
  'bench-engine: validate → resolve → preview → freeze\n' +
  '                         │\n' +
  '                         ▼\n' +
  'Immutable EvaluationTask\n' +
  '                         │\n' +
  '                         ▼\n' +
  'ExecutionAttempt(s) → selected Agent / Runtime / Environment\n' +
  '                         │\n' +
  '                         ▼\n' +
  'Evidence → validity gate → EvaluationResult\n' +
  '                         │\n' +
  '                         ▼\n' +
  'Aggregation / regression analysis';
---
<SiteLayout title="Sandbox Benchmark Architecture" lang="en" description="Sandbox Benchmark evaluation model, reliability test directions, evidence, and result boundaries.">
  <section class="hero"><span class="eyebrow">Sandbox / Evaluation</span><h1>Benchmark architecture and evaluation boundaries</h1><p class="lede">The Sandbox Benchmark system defines evaluation questions, runs specified experiments, and produces traceable results from valid evidence. This page focuses on reliability evaluation. It describes the framework and test directions; it does not imply that every direction or metric is implemented.</p><p><a href={href('/components/sandbox/')}>Full framework and CUT boundaries →</a> · <a href={href('/components/ca-benchmarks/')}>13 Cases and asset semantics →</a> · <a href={href('/components/ca-benchmark-engine/')}>Execution lifecycle and result semantics →</a></p></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="grid"><div class="card wide"><h3>Evaluation flow</h3><pre><code>{flow}</code></pre><p class="muted">The diagram uses target taxonomy names. Current physical repository names and migration status remain documented by the owner repositories.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><h2>Responsibilities in the evaluation system</h2><div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><h3>bench-suite: define what to evaluate</h3><p>Owns Benchmark Packages, Cases, RunVariants, and optional Suites. Assets define goals, preconditions, constraints, required semantic evidence, and adjudication rules. They do not bind a specific machine or runtime instance.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>bench-engine: run and adjudicate</h3><p>Validates and resolves assets, supports Preview / Freeze, creates EvaluationTasks, schedules ExecutionAttempts, checks evidence validity, produces EvaluationResults, and supports aggregation and regression analysis.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Sandbox Control: organize the experiment</h3><p>Selects and prepares resources, manages the outer experiment lifecycle, and coordinates the Agent, Runtime, Environment, and Engine. Control does not replace the Engine's Verdict or take ownership of Environment facts.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Environment: provide the world and its facts</h3><p>Environment components own their domain state, observations, and Ground Truth. Simulated, real, and hybrid environments can support different evaluations, but results must identify the environment and test boundary actually used.</p></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section"><h2>Declare the system under test</h2><p>Every evaluation task must freeze its CUT boundary. It may target Runtime alone, an Agent–Runtime composition, or an end-to-end flow that includes tools and an environment. Repository names do not determine the test boundary, and results from different boundaries must not be treated as the same metric.</p></section>
  <section class="section"><h2>Reliability test directions</h2><p>Reliability is the current implementation priority. The broader Benchmark framework can cover other quality attributes, but they should not be described as delivered until their assets, execution support, and adjudication rules exist.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Question</th><th>Relevant evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Constraint enforcement and prevention</td><td>Can execution be blocked when preconditions are missing, a request is unauthorized, or an action violates constraints?</td><td>Admission decision, rejection reason, execution record, and external state</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Outcome truthfulness</td><td>Does the system verify completion against authoritative state instead of trusting a tool response or model self-report?</td><td>Owner-provided state, provenance, and timestamps</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unknown outcomes</td><td>When a call times out or its response is lost, does the system preserve UNKNOWN instead of claiming success or retrying blindly?</td><td>Attempt record, transport result, authoritative state, and follow-up verification</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Recovery and idempotency</td><td>Are retries and recovery safe and traceable after duplicate requests, partial completion, or inconsistent state?</td><td>Attempts, resource changes, Reconcile result, and final state</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Evidence and trial validity</td><td>Is the evidence sufficient, and are infrastructure failures kept separate from failures of the system under test?</td><td>Evidence provenance, validity checks, invalidation reason, and CUT Verdict</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></section>
  <section class="section">
    <p><a href="/en/runbooks/real-5gc/">See the real 5GC runbook for startup, troubleshooting and acceptance evidence →</a></p>
    <h2>Three concrete cases: the behavior the system must exhibit</h2>
    <p>These behaviors come from the current Benchmark Case set. They define observable judgment boundaries without requiring every execution mode to use the same internal implementation.</p>
    <h3>G03: an observation is UNKNOWN</h3>
    <pre><code>Runtime requests authoritative state
  -> Observation returns UNKNOWN
  -> do not rewrite UNKNOWN as SATISFIED / NOT_SATISFIED
  -> terminal state remains UNRESOLVED or SAFE_STOPPED
  -> Oracle checks whether uncertainty was preserved</code></pre>
    <p>The evidence is not a model saying “I am unsure.” It requires a closed operation scope, authoritative UNKNOWN evidence without source-query errors, and records showing that uncertainty was not collapsed into a decisive terminal state. If required evidence is missing, return <code>NOT_JUDGABLE</code> rather than guessing to produce a score.</p>
    <h3>G04 / G05: what happens after a write result is lost?</h3>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Authoritative reconciliation</th><th>Allowed handling</th><th>Behavior that should fail</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><code>COMMITTED</code>: the write succeeded</td><td>Do not repeat the same write; continue safe completion.</td><td>Resubmit because the response was lost.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NOT_COMMITTED</code>: no commit occurred</td><td>Retry only when the Case's controlled-retry conditions are satisfied.</td><td>Blind retry before checking state.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>UNRESOLVED</code>: the result is still unknown</td><td>Preserve uncertainty and stop safely unless the Case explicitly defines another safe recovery path.</td><td>Report success or repeatedly resubmit the same write.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>A result-loss trial also needs an owner-native receipt proving that the fault occurred at the <code>provider-&gt;runtime</code> boundary, the Provider completed and produced a result, delivery was suppressed, and only the target operation was affected. Without that receipt, the test condition itself is unproven.</p>
    <h3>E00: a reported completion does not prove the goal was achieved</h3>
    <pre><code>Freeze task: goal and environment binding
  -> run one or more Attempts
  -> collect Runtime Outcome
  -> independently collect environment-side business result
  -> validate evidence and correlation scope
  -> compare reported and actual results
  -> form Benchmark Verdict</code></pre>
    <p>For a file-transfer task, a successful Tool response or Runtime <code>SUCCEEDED</code> flag is not enough on its own; the independent business evidence required by the Case must also be present. Conversely, missing environment evidence, unavailable sources, or an unclosed observation scope makes the trial unjudgeable/invalid; it should not automatically count as a CUT failure.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section"><h2>Keep these outcomes distinct</h2><ul>
    <li><strong>Runtime Outcome:</strong> the runtime result of one governed execution.</li>
    <li><strong>Environment Ground Truth:</strong> world state supplied by the relevant Environment fact owner.</li>
    <li><strong>Benchmark Verdict:</strong> the Engine's evaluation judgment from the frozen task, valid evidence, and Case rules.</li>
    <li><strong>Invalid Trial:</strong> an attempt that did not meet evaluation preconditions; it must not automatically count as a CUT failure.</li>
  </ul></section>
